[1m# Footnotes[0m

A footnote reference shows the number of
its note[1]. Labels can be numbers[2] or
words, and the numbers follow the first
reference, so the label [36m1[0m gets the
number 2. A second reference to the same
note[1] shows the same number.

A reference in [1mbold text[3][0m keeps the
style of the text, and a table cell can
hold one too:

[1mFeature[0m   │ [1mNotes[0m
──────────┼─────────────────────────
Footnotes │ A reference in a cell[4]

A reference without a
definition[^missing] stays as written.

[2m────────────────────────────────────────[0m

[2m1. [0mThis definition is long enough to
   wrap at every width of the golden
   files, and its wrapped lines start at
   the item text, not at the number.

[2m2. [0mA numeric label, defined in the
   middle of the document.

[2m3. [0mThe number is bold here.

[2m4. [0mA note with two paragraphs and a code
   block.

   The second paragraph is indented like
   the first one:

   termd --width [38;2;165;214;255m40[0m doc.md
